            "The warning: see “that no root of bitterness springs up,” and that no one is “unholy like Esau, who sold his birthright for a single meal” (12:15-16). Go back to that day in Genesis 25. Esau comes in from the field, exhausted. The smell of Jacob's red lentil stew is drifting from the fire. “Let me eat some of that red stew, for I am exhausted!” Jacob names his price, and Esau says, “I am about to die; of what use is a birthright to me?” He ate, drank, rose and went his way. “Thus Esau despised his birthright” (Genesis 25:29-34).",
